, discarding W1, which is a terminal, but if I need to form a Seven Pairs, maybe keeping the terminals is better. \n\nWait, Seven Pairs requires seven pairs. Currently, I have B5 (two) as the only pair. The rest are singles. So to get seven pairs, I need to form six more pairs. That's unlikely unless I can pair up other tiles. For example, if I have two B5s, and maybe pair up W1, W5, W7, W9, T4, T5, T6, B1, B2, B7, F2, F3. But that's 12 different tiles. To form seven pairs, I need to have seven pairs from these. It's not possible unless some of them can be paired. For example, if I have two of the same tile. But I only have B5 as two. So maybe Seven Pairs is not feasible. \n\nAnother possibility: All Chows. To have four chows and a pair. Let's see. T4-5-6 is a chow. If I can form another chow in another suit. For example, in Dots: B1-2-3? But I have B1 and B2, but not B3. Not possible. In Characters: W1-2-3? I have W1, but not W2 or W3. Not possible. So maybe only one chow. Not enough. \n\nWhat about a Full Flush? Need all tiles in one suit. My hand has multiple suits, so not possible. \n\nHmm. Maybe the best approach is to discard a tile that's not needed for any potential meld. For example, F2 or F3. Let's see. If I discard F2, then my hand has F3 left. If I discard F3, then F2 is left. Either way, I still have an honor tile. But maybe that's okay. \n\nAlternatively, discard a terminal like W1 or W9. For example, if I discard W1, then I can focus on other tiles. But W1 is a terminal, and if I'm aiming for All Terminals, but I don't have enough. \n\nLooking at the valid actions, the possible plays are to discard one of the listed tiles. The key is to choose the tile that's least needed for potential melds. \n\nB5 is duplicated. If I discard one B5, I still have one left. Maybe that's better than discarding a unique tile. For example, if I discard B5, then my hand has B5 once, which could be part of a pair if I get another. But if I discard a unique tile like W1, I 