l meld. T2 is a single, so discarding it might be the best. Let me check the valid actions: Play T2 is an option. If I discard T2, then I can focus on forming the Chows in Bamboo. Let's see:\\n\\nAfter discarding T2, my hand would be:\\n\\nW1, T4, T5, T6, T7, B1, B2, B5, B7, F3.\\n\\nNow, I can try to form [T4, T5, T6] and [T5, T6, T7], but again overlapping. So only one of them. Let's say [T4, T5, T6], then T7 is left. Then need another meld. Maybe B1, B2, B5? Not a valid Chow. Or B5, B7, but not consecutive. Hmm.\\n\\nAlternatively, maybe form [T5, T6, T7], then have T4 left. Then need another meld. Still stuck.\\n\\nThis suggests that my current hand isn't very strong. Maybe I need to look for other possibilities, like Seven Pairs or something else. Let's check.\\n\\nSeven Pairs requires seven pairs. I have:\\n\\nLooking at my current hand: W1, T2, T4, T5, T6, T7, B1, B2, B5, B7, F3. All singles except none. So no pairs. So that's not possible.\\n\\nWhat about All Terminals and Honors? No, I have suited tiles.\\n\\nWhat about All Chows? If I can form four Chows and a pair. Let's see:\\n\\n- [W5, W6, W7] (Chow)\\n- [T4, T5, T6] (Chow)\\n- [T5, T6, T7] (Chow) \\u2013 but overlapping tiles. Not possible.\\n- Maybe another Chow in Dots? B1, B2, B5, B7. Not possible.\\n\\nHmm. Maybe not.\\n\\nAlternatively, maybe a Mixed Straight? Three Chows in different suits for 1-2-3, 4-5-6, 7-8-9. But I don't have the necessary tiles.\\n\\nThis is getting complicated. Maybe the best move is to discard a tile that's not part of any potential meld. T2 is a single, so discarding it would be the best. Let's go with Play T2.\\n\", \"output\": \"Evaluation: [The current hand has limited potential for high-scoring patterns. The best approach is to discard a tile that doesn't contribute to forming melds.]\\nReason: [Discarding T2 (Bamboo 2) removes a single tile not involved in any potential melds, preserving flexibility for forming Chows in Bamboo or other suits.]\\nAnswer: Play T2\"}"}
