 No, only one T5. Not enough.\\n\\nHmm. Let's think about the possible pairs. The possible pairs in my hand are T7 (two copies) or maybe B2. So if I can form a pair of T7s, then the remaining tiles would need to form three melds. Let's see:\\n\\nIf the pair is T7, then the remaining tiles are W6, T3, T4, T5, T6, T7, B2. Wait, no, the pair is T7, so we remove two T7s. So remaining tiles: W6, T3, T4, T5, T6, B2. Now, need to form three melds. Let's see:\\n\\nPossible melds:\\n\\n- T3, T4, T5 (Chow)\\n- T5, T6, and need T7 for a Chow, but T7 is already in the pair. So not possible.\\n- W6 could be part of a Chow if there's W5 or W7, but the existing meld already has [W5, W6, W7]. So W6 is already in a meld. Wait, no\\u2014the W6 in my hand is separate from the meld. Wait, the melds are already formed. The hand tiles are the ones not in melds. So the W6 in my hand is separate from the [W5, W6, W7] meld. So I can use the W6 in my hand to form another meld. Wait, but how? If I have W6 in my hand, can I form a Pung or Chow with it? Let's see. To form a Pung, I need two more W6s. I have one in hand, and the unshown tiles show W6: 2 remaining. So if I can draw a W6, maybe. But I need to consider current actions.\\n\\nAlternatively, maybe I can form a Chow with W6 and other tiles. But I don't have W5 or W7 in my hand. The existing meld has them, but I can't use them again. So the W6 in my hand is standalone.\\n\\nSo, if I have the pair of T7s, then the remaining tiles are W6, T3, T4, T5, T6, B2. Need three melds. Let's see:\\n\\n- T3, T4, T5 (Chow)\\n- T5, T6, and need T7 (but T7 is in the pair)\\n- W6: can't form a meld\\n- B2: can't form a meld\\n\\nSo that's only one meld. Not enough. Therefore, maybe the pair isn't T7.\\n\\nAlternative pair: B2. If the pair is B2, then remaining tiles are W6, T3, T4, T5, T6, T7 (two). Need three melds.\\n\\nPossible melds:\\n\\n- T3, T4, T5 (Chow)\\n- T5, T6, T7 (Chow)\\n- W6: can't form a meld\\n\\nSo that's two melds. Still not enough. 